just in the last week or so there's been  a couple of interesting stories from  space um both involving water in space  in one way or another so we thought we'd  have a quick chat and  tell you about those  so the first one is is the classic water  story in some ways which is water on  mars it's something that people have  been fascinated about for centuries i  guess when people first thought there  might be canals on mars then that  introduced the idea there might be water  on mars and the fascination obviously  fundamentally arises from the fact that  we think that water is sort of  fundamentally associated with life and  there's the best evidence today of  liquid water on the surface of mars has  just been released there's a space pro  out there at the moment the mars  reconnaissance orbiter nasa probe which  has been taking pictures of the  surface of mars for some years now  martian years now and one of the things  that they've recently discovered is that  on some hillsides on mars in winter  there's nothing much going on and then  in the martian spring you start finding  these little sort of dark streaks  appearing on these hillsides and as you  follow them through the summer months  you find the streaks get longer and  longer and sometimes they bifurcate they  split into two sometimes they merge  together and for all the world they look  like little trickles of water running  down the hillside this is water now that  you can see running on the surface of  mars which means that you know when it's  it's not there on the surface it must be  very close to the surface and so the  idea now is that if you're finding this  this much water on the surface if you  could actually dig down a bit then  potentially there could be great  reservoirs of the stuff down there there  is a one interesting issue which is that  if you kind of figure out what the  surface temperature and atmospheric  pressure and so on on mars is likely to  be  there shouldn't really be any liquid  water on the surface it should still be  frozen it's very cold still  and so the only way you can actually  make it  that there is actually liquid water on  the surface of mars is if it isn't pure  water if it has to be brine it has to be  very salty water because if you add salt  to water it lowers the freezing  temperature considerably and although  salty water is not very conducive to  life there are actually quite a lot of  bacteria here on earth that really  thrive in a salty environment and so the  idea is that if you're really looking  for life on mars the best bet at the  moment is that they're likely to be  these sort of salt loving bacteria  living in these trickles of water how  come we're never catching the smoking  gun it just seems too weird that we  never actually see it we always just  missed it by a few hours i think it's at  least in part that people are very  conservative about where they land  things because you want to make sure  when you land a probe on mars that it's  not going to tip over when it lands that  it's not going to fall down a ravine and  so on so people tend to pick fairly dull  places to land and not surprisingly the  exciting stuff like running water and so  on tends to happen in the hilly places  and the more exciting places so as we  get a bit more adventurous i'll start  building probes that can actually travel  larger distances on mars i think we will  actually find these things but it is  just this effect that you tend to be a  bit conservative where you put them down  which means that you tend to pick the  dole places so not surprisingly the  place turns out looking a little bit  dull as well  and how come whenever they see evidence  of liquid flowing they always say it's  water couldn't there be other liquids  that cause these little riverlets and  erosion on the sides of hills it could  be there aren't that many materials that  are actually liquid at those kind of  temperatures so there aren't a huge  number of things to choose from and  because both hydrogen and oxygen are  very abundant in the universe  the most likely thing that you're going  to find running on the surface is going  to be water it really is odds on that  they have found water now i don't think  it's not one of those things that  someone is going to come up with an  alternative explanation i suspect quite  how important it is for you know life on  mars and those kinds of things remains  to be seen but no it's an interesting  story certainly  it is related to water but actually let  me start with oxygen oxygen is a very  abundant element in the universe one of  the most abundant there is and yet  bizarrely astronomers have never  actually found molecular oxygen anywhere  in the universe  we can find it in other compounds carbon  monoxide for example exists a lot and  that's carbon and oxygen together but  oxygen on its own o2 oxygen plus oxygen  has never been found before except here  on earth yes and i was out there in  space i meant to say yes and so people  have tried to come up with explanations  as to why they can't find it and that's  where we come back to water again  because the thought is that probably a  lot of the oxygen out there is actually  locked up in water and then you can't  then see lots and lots of water because  that water is then frozen as ice and if  you take those take that ice and put it  on the surface of little grains in space  so you have these little sort of sand  grains and dust grains  that are covered in in little icy coats  then they're very chemically  non-reactive and so actually you don't  see very much going on and you're not  going to produce molecular oxygen from  those so someone came up with a clever  experiment to actually try and and  detect it for the first time which is if  this story is right and all that water  is out there so all the oxygen is locked  up in water on these dust grains if you  look at somewhere where those little icy  coats are being melted away and so it's  turned back into liquid water then when  you've got liquid water you have much  more chance of chemical reactions then  you so you can then convert some of that  oxygen from the water into molecular  oxygen this is a chance to detect it so  what the these guys have done with the  herschel space telescope the esa  mission that monitors the universe in  the infrared  is looked at some star-forming regions  for the signature of oxygen of course in  a star-forming region you've got lots of  new stars forming which are heating up  their environments which means if there  are any of these little frozen grains  out there the water will get melted on  the surface of them so the hope was well  if we look at these star forming regions  we'll actually see that water melting  away and then some of it being converted  into oxygen so we can actually detect  the oxygen  and sure enough when they actually did  the experiment they found for the first  time the clear signature of oxygen in  the data that they were taking so you  have to do spectroscopy so you have to  split the light up into a spectrum and  then because the oxygen molecules absorb  uh particular wavelengths of of light  and re-emit particular wavelengths of  light you can find those signatures in  the spectral that very particular  wavelengths of light from the spectrum  might be missing entirely or you might  have an extra excess at a particular  wavelength even though these are what  for astronomers are quite dense regions  because the stars forming there it's  still comparable to the best vacuums you  can get on earth so no no there's tiny  amounts of oxygen you certainly wouldn't  want to be out there trying to breathe

